  "worked_solution": "Recall that to determine whether $y(x)$ is a solution to the differential equation, we substitute $y$ and its derivatives into the equation. \n\nLet's check each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{1}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}^{\u2032}(x) & =-2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2x) \\\\ {y}_{1}^{\u2033}(x) & =-4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{1}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2x)+4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{1}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement II is true. First, we find the first and second derivatives of ${y}_{2}(x):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{2}^{\u2032}(x) & =-8\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2x) \\\\ {y}_{2}^{\u2033}(x) & =-16\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's substitute ${y}_{2}(x)$ and its derivatives into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-16\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2x)+16\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2x) & =0 \\\\ 0 & =0\\,\u2713\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, ${y}_{2}(x)$ is a solution to the equation.\n\n- Statement III is false. Both ${y}_{1}$ and ${y}_{2}$ are solutions of the equation. To check if these functions are linearly independent, we compute the Wronskian:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}W({y}_{1},{y}_{2}) & =|\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1} & {y}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{1}^{\u2032} & {y}_{2}^{\u2032}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2x) & 4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2x) \\\\ -2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2x) & -8\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2x)\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2x)\\cdot (-8\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2x))-4\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2x)\\cdot (-2\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2x)) \\\\  & =-8\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2x)\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2x)+8\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2x)\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2x) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the  Wronskian equals $0$ for all $x\\in R,$ the functions are not linearly independent. Hence, ${y}_{1}(x)$ and ${y}_{2}(x)$ don't form a set of fundamental solutions to the equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\".",
